WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.567
A verified signal now
reaches your sellers,

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.333
and it needs an owner in seconds.

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:08.400
Every new lead lands with a human who
owns it, chosen by rules you wrote,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.433
and every choice comes with its reason.

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:13.900
Over the next few minutes you'll
write a territory, set capacity,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.000
and read the decision
behind any assignment.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.100
Assigning an owner is an internal write.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.467
The only thing Routing sends out
is a notification to the owner.

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:25.233
Mia's demo request lands in a shared
queue. Two reps reply, or nobody does,

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:28.867
and the account's owner in
the CRM never hears about it.

00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:32.900
Speed to lead is lost to a question
that needed a rule, not a meeting.

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:34.400
First, territories.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.867
A territory is a set of criteria
with an owner or a rotation.

00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:40.333
In the console: Engines, Routing,

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.800
and the territory editor
under When routing runs.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.067
Criteria are structured,
like country or company size.

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:48.500
They match first, before any rotation.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.900
Each territory has a
priority and an on switch.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.000
Only a person edits territories.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.267
Your agent can read them
and never change them.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:59.567
Or ask your agent: "Which territory
would this lead fall into? "

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.033
Second, strategy. Routing assigns by
round robin, territory or named owner.

00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:07.133
Stickiness keeps a record
with the owner of its account,

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.967
so a new contact never splits
an account between two reps.

00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:12.367
When nobody in the
territory can take the lead,

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:15.300
overflow sends it to a fallback
owner or a named queue.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.733
It tries again after a day, or
the moment you change a setting.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.267
Third, capacity, which keeps it fair.

00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.767
In the console: Engines,
Routing, Team capacity.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:26.033
Each person has caps on open
leads and leads per week,

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:28.200
and a weight from one to a hundred.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.833
The person with the lightest
load for their weight is next.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:34.333
People can be paused or
marked out of office.

00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:36.100
Dates follow their own time zone.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.300
An owner can lock capacity for the
whole team. Availability is human only.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.033
An agent can't change it.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.333
Fourth, matching a lead to an account.

00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:47.333
In the console: Engines,
Routing, match preview.

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:50.967
A lead on the exact company
domain attaches automatically.

00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:53.933
A subdomain or a sibling
domain is only a suggestion,

00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:57.533
unless the territory allows it, and
free mail addresses never match.

00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.133
The preview shows the result
before anything is assigned.

00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:05.167
Or ask your agent: "Which account does
this lead match, and who owns it? "

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.333
Now the part that earns trust.

00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:10.500
Every assignment writes a decision
record: assigned, kept or queued,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.400
the territory and rule that fired,
the reason, and who or what did it.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.200
In the console: Engines,
Routing, Assignment history,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.433
and its Decision column.

00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:23.000
So when someone asks why a lead
went to Leo, you read the answer.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.633
Or ask your agent: "Who
owns this lead and why? "

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.733
Owners hear about it by Slack or email.
A notification leaves the building,

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:33.167
so it follows Routing's
own Dry or Live switch.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:37.067
In Dry Mode you can watch who would
have been told, without telling anyone.

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:37.800
Pro move:

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.300
stay in Dry for a week and read

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.700
the Decision column before
the first real ping.

00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:44.667
Speed to lead puts it
together. When a form comes in,

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.933
the play routes the lead without
taking it from an existing owner.

00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:52.900
It skips customers, creates a first
touch task due in fifteen minutes,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.500
and drafts a reply with the
send proposed for approval.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.933
In the console: Engines,
Plays, speed to lead, Simulate.

00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:02.967
Or ask your agent: "Simulate speed
to lead over the last thirty days".

00:03:02.967 --> 00:03:06.133
You'll see who it would have
assigned, with zero writes.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.633
Routing doesn't tune itself, so you do.

00:03:08.633 --> 00:03:11.967
Read the Decision column, find
where leads queue or overflow,

00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:13.400
then adjust territories and caps.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.100
Or ask your agent: "Find unowned
leads and preview owners".

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.233
A scheduled reconcile play and the routing

00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:21.833
steward agent check hourly
for leads nobody owns.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.200
Routing is the receiver.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.533
The qualified handoff
from Score and the signal

00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:27.867
reaction from Signals
both ask it for an owner.

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:30.833
Outreach then uses that owner
when a reply comes back.

00:03:30.867 --> 00:03:31.867
What's still missing:

00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:35.200
response time targets with
escalation when an owner doesn't act,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.167
and a scorecard for Routing of its own.

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:40.600
Today speed to lead is the first
touch task and the play results,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.667
not a clock that Routing enforces.

00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:46.600
A careful engineer will say: an agent
will reshuffle my accounts overnight.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.467
It can't edit territories or availability,
because those are human only.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:53.033
Stickiness keeps a record
with its account's owner.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:55.900
And every move it does make
writes a decision you can read.

00:03:55.933 --> 00:04:00.067
Checkpoint: Territories match
first, capacity keeps the load fair,

00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:01.867
and stickiness keeps accounts whole.

00:04:01.867 --> 00:04:04.667
One owner per lead, and
the reason is written down.

00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:08.533
Every lead reaches the right owner in
seconds, and the reason is on record.

00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:11.600
Read yesterday's decisions
under Engines, Routing.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:12.867
After this comes Outreach,

00:04:12.867 --> 00:04:16.100
where that owner sends nothing
until a second person approves.
